# docs/NATIVE-AGENTS.md §6.1 — the decision drawer is docked to the composer, never a modal
# docs/NATIVE-AGENTS.md §6.2 — `[y] allow once` · `[a] allow for this session` · `[n] deny` ·
#   `[esc] deny and stop`, and `[e] edit` is offered by Claude and **never** by Codex
# docs/KEYMAP.md §Native agent thread — `Agent > AgentDecision > AgentPermission`
# docs/TESTING-HARNESS.md §5 — the scripted `edit-approval.json` the preset gives Codex
#
# The gate is answered with the pointer, which is what the frozen approval targets are for.
fixture: agents
await idle
key o
await screen == Workspace 20000

key ctrl-s A
await agents.threads[0].provider == codex 20000
assert lists.tabs.selected.badges[0] == codex && focused == agents.composer
await idle 30000
type apply the README fix
key enter

# The turn stops at the file-change approval. A gate owns the keyboard in the frame it appears.
await agents.threads[0].pending_gate == permission 30000
assert agents.threads[0].state == needs_you && lists.tabs.selected.marks[0] == needs_you
assert key_contexts[1] == AgentDecision && key_contexts[2] == AgentPermission
assert toasts[0].text ~= "needs permission"
shot approval
# The dump is the evidence that Codex is offered no `[e] edit`: its `targets` map holds
# `agents.approval.allow_once`, `allow_always`, `deny` and `deny_and_stop` and no `edit`. A
# predicate cannot read it — every target name contains a `.`, and the predicate path splits on
# `.` — so the assertion lives in the dump a reviewer opens, not in a clause.
dump approval

# `[y] allow once` — the drawer's own control, clicked. The gate closes and the thread stops
# needing you, which is the whole of what the answer promises.
click agents.approval.allow_once
await agents.threads[0].pending_gate absent 30000
assert agents.threads[0].state != needs_you && lists.tabs.selected.marks[0] != needs_you
await agents.threads[0].state == idle 30000
assert sticky_error absent
dump allowed
quit
